msgid "explanatory/angle/description"
msgstr ""
"The angle between the {t_2_Open}shell trajectory{t_2_Close} and the line "
"perpendicular to the armor plate."

msgid "explanatory/angle/header"
msgstr "Hit angle"

msgid "explanatory/effective/description"
msgstr ""
"The total {t_3_Open}resulting armor thickness{t_3_Close} along the shell "
"trajectory."

msgid "explanatory/effective/header"
msgstr "Effective armor"

msgid "explanatory/guide"
msgstr "Want to know more? Check out our video guide."

msgid "explanatory/header"
msgstr "Legend"

msgid "explanatory/linkButton"
msgstr "Mastering Armor"

msgid "explanatory/nominal/description"
msgstr ""
"The actual width of the {t_1_Open}main or spaced{t_1_Close} armor plate(s)."

msgid "explanatory/nominal/header"
msgstr "Nominal armor"

msgid "explanatory/note/description"
msgstr ""
"Effective armor is only slightly affected by {t_4_Open}shell type and caliber{t_4_Close}.\n"
" \n"
"In battle there are more factors affecting armor penetration: {t_5_Open}ballistics{t_5_Close}, {t_6_Open}distance to target{t_6_Close}, {t_7_Open}randomization{t_7_Close}, and {t_8_Open}ricochets{t_8_Close}."

msgid "explanatory/note/header"
msgstr "Keep in mind"

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_1/body"
msgstr ""
"A vehicle is only damaged if its main armor is hit and penetrated.\n"
"\n"
"Hitting spaced armor does not damage a vehicle unless a shell hits and then penetrates the main armor after that.\n"
"\n"
"Hitting and penetrating external modules damages those modules directly: hit a track to immobilize a vehicle, hit a gun to disable it, or hit an observation device to reduce a vehicle's view range."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_2/body"
msgstr ""
"The shell trajectory is calculated as if the shell moves in a straight line from the center of the screen.\n"
"\n"
"Hit angle does not apply to external modules (such as tracks and gun)—here, effective armor equals nominal armor.\n"
"\n"
"If there are multiple armor layers at the point of impact, the tooltip displays the hit angle for the first layer only."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_3/body"
msgstr ""
"For a single armor layer, effective armor is the nominal armor modified by the hit angle and shell normalization.\n"
"\n"
"If there are multiple armor layers at the point of impact, the result is the sum of the effective values for each layer.\n"
"\n"
"Shell normalization reduces the hit angle by 5 degrees for armor-piercing shells and by 2 degrees for APCR shells.\n"
"\n"
"Other shell types are not normalized.\n"
"\n"
"The shell normalization angle may be further increased if the shell caliber is greater than twice the armor thickness."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_4/body"
msgstr ""
"In this window, an armor-piercing shell of minimum caliber is used to calculate the effective armor.\n"
"\n"
"The effective armor value may differ slightly for other shell types and calibers."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_5/body"
msgstr ""
"The real shell trajectory is not a straight line—it curves due to gravity, which affects the hit angle.\n"
"\n"
"The effect is negligible for most vehicles, but may be noticeable for vehicles with slow shell velocity or at long distances.\n"
"\n"
"This is especially significant for SPG shots."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_6/body"
msgstr ""
"Armor-piercing and APCR shell penetration is reduced with distance. You may "
"see this in the tooltips for those shells."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_7/body"
msgstr ""
"Each time a shot is fired, shell penetration is randomized by +-25%.\n"
"\n"
"Shell direction is slightly randomized with each shot, so it hits a random spot inside the aiming circle."

msgid "explanatory/tooltips/t_8/body"
msgstr ""
"The ricochet angle is 70 degrees for armor-piercing and APCR shells and 85 degrees for HEAT shells.\n"
"\n"
"HE shells do not ricochet.\n"
"\n"
"A ricochet will not occur if the shell's caliber is greater than three times the nominal armor thickness at the point of impact (this only applies to AP and APCR shells)."

